WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.970
Every morning my phone

2
00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.650
says, “Update ready.”

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.280
Every morning I press “Later”.

4
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.180
It works fine, so why bother?

5
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.960
Today: what an update really fixes, and

6
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.640
what to do when the updates stop.

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.140
Every app and every system has

8
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.640
small mistakes in its code.

9
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.740
Some of them are holes a

10
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.240
criminal can use to get in.

11
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.140
When the maker finds one, they send a

12
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.320
fix: that’s a security update.

13
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.780
But once the fix is out, attackers

14
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.120
study it, and go looking for phones

15
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.620
that haven’t installed it.

16
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.300
It’s like a broken lock on

17
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.280
the school store room.

18
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.390
Once everyone hears which lock is broken,

19
00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.690
that’s the first door a thief tries.

20
00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.370
An update is the new lock.

21
00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.090
Every day you wait, the door stays open.

22
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.110
Turn on automatic updates, for the

23
00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.230
phone’s system and for your apps.

24
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.770
Let them run at night, on the charger and

25
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.130
on Wi-Fi, so they don’t eat your data.

26
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.820
And when it asks to restart, say

27
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.180
yes, because many fixes only start

28
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.420
working after a restart.

29
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.260
Quick check.

30
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.980
My phone says an update is ready, but

31
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.300
I’m teaching all morning and my

32
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.040
battery is at forty per cent.

33
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.900
What should I do?

34
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.180
Tonight, on the charger and on Wi-Fi.

35
00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:28.690
Not in the middle of a

36
00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.450
lesson, and not “never”.

37
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.790
Most phones let you choose overnight

38
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.930
updates, so you only decide once.

39
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.660
Updates don’t last forever.

40
00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.550
One day, every phone and

41
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.090
computer stops getting them.

42
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.550
Look in Settings for the security update

43
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:49.270
date, under About phone or Software

44
00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:49.730
update.

45
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.750
If it’s more than a year old and no

46
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.190
update is waiting, your phone has

47
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.870
probably stopped getting fixes.

48
00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.410
Many school computers

49
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.730
still run Windows ten.

50
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.340
It left normal support in

51
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.900
October twenty twenty-five.

52
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.030
Since then, it gets security updates only

53
00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.270
through Microsoft’s Extended Security

54
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.310
Updates: home computers until October

55
00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.790
twenty twenty-seven, and organisations

56
00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:16.650
pay.

57
00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.470
Otherwise, move it to Windows eleven

58
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.470
or another supported system.

59
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.290
Until you can, keep it away from

60
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.670
email, banking and pupils’ records.

61
00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.070
Updates only protect what’s real.

62
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.700
Get apps from the official store,

63
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.540
never from a link in a chat.

64
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.600
Say no to “modded” apps and cracked

65
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.960
software: free copies of paid

66
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.700
programs often hide malware.

67
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.580
And be careful with USB sticks

68
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.200
passed around at the cyber café.

69
00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:48.270
Your turn.

70
00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.570
A colleague posts in the staff group:

71
00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.190
“Free full version of the timetable

72
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.890
program, no licence needed!”

73
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.530
Safe or risky?

74
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.620
Risky: cracked software often carries

75
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.380
malware, even when a colleague shares it.

76
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.880
Remember: automatic updates, restart

77
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.380
when asked, check the security date,

78
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.780
and use only the official store.

79
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.860
Try it in class tomorrow: read out six

80
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.140
short situations, like an update during an

81
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.980
exam, or a cracked game from a friend.

82
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.170
Pupils hold up green for safe and

83
00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.330
red for risky, and explain why.
